<html>
<title>Code Coverage</title>
<body>


<h1>Code Coverage</h1>

<p>
Code that is not executed is shown as data, as in the <a href="DirectMain.htm">Direct Assembler</a> - in a <font color="#3947CE">blue</font> colour, displayed as bytes including ASCII characters (1):
</p>

<p>
<img src="images/profiler_code_coverage.png" border="0" width="724" height="609">
</p>
<ul>
<li>(1) Unexecuted code shown as data bytes</li>
</ul>

<p>
The parts shown as data may, of course, be actual data used by the software, or code that has not yet been executed. The branch at 0185 checking EF3 is currently always branching to 0191. In this case the running computer (COMX-35) uses EF3 as an indication of a key press, so testing this piece of software indicates that a key needs to be pressed to execute all code. Pressing a key results in the following:
</p>

<p>
<img src="images/profiler_code_coverage_key_pressed.png" border="0" width="724" height="609">
</p>
<ul>
<li>(1) Previously unexecuted code now executed</li>
</ul>

<p>
The result shows that all code on this page has now been executed.
</p>

<p>
<strong>Note:</strong> The blue bytes at the bottom are clearly data, as are the following blue bytes. The <tt>SEP R4</tt> at 01A2 likely reads the value <tt>14</tt> from 01A3, and the <tt>OUT 3</tt> uses <tt>20</tt> as the output value.
</p>
</body>
</html>
